WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.580
Hey, welcome to Fascinating Frontiers Episode 129 for July 12th, 2026.

00:00:06.380 --> 00:00:09.840
Let's look at what's new across the frontiers of space and science.

00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:15.200
UN records now let anyone track satellite registrations that official sites still hide.

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:22.200
Today's update moves the flight test cadence forward and keeps the open question of reuse intervals in view.

00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:27.620
SpaceX has begun preparations for the 13th integrated flight test of Starship.

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:32.460
The vehicle could launch as early as July 16th from Starbase in Texas.

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:38.240
Engineers will watch propellant transfer performance and booster catch attempts on this flight.

00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:42.740
The mission profile includes multiple engine burns after separation.

00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:46.720
Data from this flight will inform the next set of hardware changes.

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:52.300
Now on a completely different note, Orbital Analyst Jonathan McDowell reports that

00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:58.280
fresh satellite registration documents remain unavailable through the standard UNOSCA website.

00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:04.740
The same files appear on the main UN documents portal when the correct document ID string is entered.

00:01:05.560 --> 00:01:10.320
Access still requires manual guessing of identifiers rather than a searchable index.

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:16.420
Older records have also become reachable after a tip from another user on how to navigate the archive.

00:01:17.220 --> 00:01:22.860
The workaround restores visibility into recent launches without waiting for an official interface update.

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:28.720
Better launch visibility pairs naturally with new questions about what those launches might encounter.

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:36.860
Researchers from Italy and Germany have modeled a lightweight permanent magnet array that could deflect solar energetic particles.

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:43.080
The design avoids the power demands of superconducting coils and the mass penalty of water shielding.

00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:50.200
Computer simulations show the static field can reduce crew exposure during solar storms by a measurable fraction.

00:01:50.980 --> 00:01:55.860
The team's pre-print explores field geometry needed to cover a small habitat module.

00:01:56.540 --> 00:02:00.200
The magnets would be mounted externally on the spacecraft structure.

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:03.540
No cooling systems or electrical current are required.

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:07.560
Radiation protection matters most on long voyages.

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:13.200
One recent x-ray study shows how uneven forces already shape outcomes in space.

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:19.140
A neutron star squeezes the mass of the sun into a sphere roughly 20 kilometers across.

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:23.740
A teaspoon of its material would weigh as much as a mountain on earth.

00:02:24.140 --> 00:02:29.560
Permanent magnets proposed for spacecraft create a comparable but far weaker static field

00:02:29.560 --> 00:02:35.060
that can nudge charged particles away from a crew module without any electrical power.

00:02:35.060 --> 00:02:39.980
Picture riding alongside a solar energetic particle as it approaches the habitat.

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:46.820
Instead of a straight path, the proton curves along the field lines and misses the thin shell by tens of meters.

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:52.780
The geometry that works best is a simple dipole whose poles sit outside the living quarters.

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:59.840
Even a modest field of a few Tesla can reduce the dose from a typical solar storm by 30 to 40 percent

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:05.660
according to the new simulations. The remaining puzzle is whether the same arrangement leaves gaps

00:03:05.660 --> 00:03:11.880
at the cusps near the magnetic poles. Dutch astronomers used Chandra x-ray observatory data

00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:20.680
to track the shock front of G292.0 plus 1.8. The remnant rich in oxygen shows faster expansion

00:03:20.680 --> 00:03:27.280
along some axis than others. The asymmetry suggests the original explosion was not perfectly spherical.

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:32.400
Continued monitoring will test whether the pattern persists as the remnant ages.

00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:37.140
The x-ray images resolve the forward shock in multiple energy bands.

00:03:37.900 --> 00:03:42.000
Proper motion measurements over several years reveal the velocity differences.

00:03:42.680 --> 00:03:46.420
The remnant lies several thousand light years away in the southern sky.

00:03:47.060 --> 00:03:52.620
From one kind of cosmic asymmetry, we turn to objects that push the distance record even farther.

00:03:53.260 --> 00:03:58.880
Astronomers have identified two quasars farther from Earth than any previously confirmed objects.

00:03:59.560 --> 00:04:05.300
The discovery relied on spectroscopic follow-up of candidates selected from wide field surveys.

00:04:06.180 --> 00:04:10.720
Both objects are powered by supermassive black holes accruing at high rates.

00:04:11.420 --> 00:04:17.120
Their light provides new probes of conditions in the universe less than a billion years after the big bang.

00:04:17.960 --> 00:04:21.920
The spectra show strong emission lines shifted far into the infrared.

00:04:22.620 --> 00:04:25.800
Additional candidates from the same survey are under review.

00:04:26.480 --> 00:04:30.520
Early universe extremes contrast with a very recent human milestone.

00:04:31.240 --> 00:04:36.880
Harvard astronomer Avi Loeb has been appointed to head a fresh federal study group on unidentified

00:04:36.880 --> 00:04:43.300
aerial phenomena. The panel will review sensor data and witness reports with an emphasis on scientific

00:04:43.300 --> 00:04:49.880
methods. Loeb previously led the Galileo project which searches for technological signatures from

00:04:49.880 --> 00:04:54.860
interstellar objects. The new group's first report is expected within a year.

00:04:55.640 --> 00:05:00.180
The appointment brings an astronomer with experience in anomaly detection to the effort.

00:05:01.060 --> 00:05:04.600
Data sources will include both government and civilian instruments.

00:05:05.140 --> 00:05:09.220
Official scrutiny of anomalies sits alongside quieter national ambitions.

00:05:09.760 --> 00:05:16.240
Pakistan has formally asked NASA to explore cooperation on a lunar landing targeted for 2035.

00:05:16.880 --> 00:05:22.100
The proposal includes shared technology development and possible joint science instruments.

00:05:22.780 --> 00:05:26.340
Discussions would focus on lander systems and surface operations.

00:05:26.920 --> 00:05:32.040
The request marks the country's first public step toward independent crude lunar capability.

00:05:32.700 --> 00:05:38.500
No formal agreement has been signed yet. The approach would build on existing bilateral space science

00:05:38.500 --> 00:05:43.540
exchanges. New partners look outward while earlier spacefarers are remembered.

00:05:44.180 --> 00:05:51.460
Alexander Alexandrov, who flew under the intercosmos program in 1988, passed away on July 11th.

00:05:51.800 --> 00:05:56.280
He became the second Bulgarian citizen to reach orbit after Georgi Ivanov.

00:05:57.340 --> 00:06:03.580
Alexanderov's mission on Soyuz TM-5 included earth observation and material science experiments.

00:06:04.320 --> 00:06:09.060
Colleagues remember him for his work training later crews in microgravity procedures.

00:06:09.620 --> 00:06:12.920
His flight lasted eight days and returned safely to earth.

00:06:13.200 --> 00:06:16.960
Alexanderov remained active in space education after retirement.

00:06:17.820 --> 00:06:21.620
Human stories of survival lead into documented close calls.

00:06:22.320 --> 00:06:27.480
A new feature lists eight documented incidents in which astronauts survived life-threatening

00:06:27.480 --> 00:06:34.200
failures during flight. The cases span multiple nations and decades. Each example includes the

00:06:34.200 --> 00:06:38.300
specific system failure that occurred and the steps crews took to recover.

00:06:39.140 --> 00:06:44.200
The article draws on mission transcripts and post-flight reports rather than speculation.

00:06:45.080 --> 00:06:50.200
Readers see the exact sequence of events that turned potential disasters into safer turns.

00:06:50.800 --> 00:06:54.040
The incidents cover both American and Soviet era missions.

00:06:54.480 --> 00:06:58.940
From crude close calls, we move to an entirely different kind of tracking challenge.

00:06:59.500 --> 00:07:05.840
A new caller combines GPS with SpaceX's Direct to Cell Starlink network to maintain contact

00:07:05.840 --> 00:07:11.980
anywhere on earth. The device can send location updates and short messages even when traditional

00:07:11.980 --> 00:07:18.180
cellular coverage is absent. Developers say the caller will enter limited production later this year.

00:07:18.660 --> 00:07:23.900
It is the first commercial pet product to rely on the emerging satellite to phone constellation.

00:07:24.700 --> 00:07:29.260
The hardware uses the same frequency bands planned for future smartphone connections.

00:07:29.920 --> 00:07:33.340
Battery life supports multi-day tracking without recharging.

00:07:33.920 --> 00:07:39.800
Every day technology now reaches remote places. Similar persistence appears in amateur astronomy.

00:07:40.500 --> 00:07:49.360
A Nikon Z8 paired with a Takahashi TSA 120 telescope recorded 5,400 raw frames of the lunar surface.

00:07:49.920 --> 00:07:55.060
The sequence was aligned and stacked to produce a short time lapse showing changing shadows across

00:07:55.060 --> 00:08:02.680
craters. The mount used was a ZWO AM 5-inch high frame rate mode at 20 frames per second.

00:08:03.300 --> 00:08:09.680
Processing combined PIPP auto-stackered and Photoshop before final animation in After Effects.

00:08:10.460 --> 00:08:13.680
The best 10% of frames were selected for the final stack.

00:08:14.380 --> 00:08:18.760
The animation reveals subtle topographic relief under changing illumination.

00:08:19.360 --> 00:08:23.660
Ground-based imaging continues with deep sky targets under city skies.

00:08:24.360 --> 00:08:33.400
From Baghdad, a C-star S-50 telescope collected 12 hours of luminance data on NGC-6960 under light

00:08:33.400 --> 00:08:40.480
pollution filters. The resulting 1.3 times mosaic reveals fine filamentary structure in the supernova

00:08:40.480 --> 00:08:45.660
remnant. Stacking and processing were completed in picks inside and Photoshop.

00:08:45.660 --> 00:08:51.040
The image demonstrates that useful deep sky data can be gathered from an urban site.

00:08:51.500 --> 00:08:58.640
The veil complex lies about 1500 light-years away in sickness. The observer used an EQ

00:08:58.640 --> 00:09:04.740
mode mount to track the field. One more dedicated observer closes our look at recent records.

00:09:05.320 --> 00:09:14.040
A 200mm Newtonian telescope gathered 26 hours of luminance, 7 hours of RGB, and 2.5 hours of

00:09:14.040 --> 00:09:23.200
H-alpha on NGC-6966. Two cooled CMOS cameras operating at minus 15 degrees Celsius captured

00:09:23.200 --> 00:09:30.060
the data from a portal for location. The final image shows star forming regions and dust lanes across

00:09:30.060 --> 00:09:35.820
the spiral arms. Processing used picks insight, steady astrotools, and Photoshop.

00:09:36.360 --> 00:09:40.300
The galaxy lies roughly 25 million light-years away in sefius.

00:09:41.020 --> 00:09:46.360
The long luminance exposure brings out faint outer structures. The H-alpha data highlight

00:09:46.360 --> 00:09:52.980
active star-forming complexes. Polarization measurements add another layer to x-ray views of compact

00:09:52.980 --> 00:09:59.640
objects. The imaging x-ray polarometry explorer has mapped the magnetic field geometry around a

00:09:59.640 --> 00:10:06.040
pulsar nicknamed the lighthouse. Polarization measurements show the field lines twist in ways that

00:10:06.040 --> 00:10:12.380
match theoretical predictions for a rotating neutron star. The data cover multiple rotation phases

00:10:12.380 --> 00:10:18.740
and provide the first direct view of the emission region's orientation. Follow-up observations are

00:10:18.740 --> 00:10:24.940
planned to test whether the pattern changes over months. The pulsar emits beams that sweep across

00:10:24.940 --> 00:10:32.140
the line of sight like a lighthouse. IXPE's detectors record the angle of polarized x-rays from each

00:10:32.140 --> 00:10:38.860
phase. The results constrain models of the magnetosphere. Planetary surfaces also reveal ongoing

00:10:38.860 --> 00:10:46.100
processes that leave no lasting trace. In the planet's polar regions, carbon dioxide freezes and precipitates

00:10:46.100 --> 00:10:52.840
as fine crystals that sublimate before reaching the surface. NASA's Marsus radar has mapped layers

00:10:52.840 --> 00:10:59.320
of this dry ice several kilometers thick. Beneath the ice, the same instrument has detected what appears

00:10:59.320 --> 00:11:05.540
to be liquid water that has persisted for billions of years. The snowfall occurs only where temperatures

00:11:05.540 --> 00:11:12.760
drop below minus 125 degrees Celsius. The radar reflections distinguish the ice layers from underlying

00:11:12.760 --> 00:11:19.920
rock and liquid. No surface deposit forms because the crystals disappear in the thin atmosphere. The

00:11:19.920 --> 00:11:28.760
process repeats each Martian winter. Next time, we'll be watching for the first report from the new

